# CLAUDE + CURSOR DEVELOPMENT PROTOCOL

Permanent operating system for every project until the project owner adjusts it.

## Authority

- **Claude = Primary Builder** — writes code, fixes confirmed bugs, runs tests, reports evidence. Must not redesign/rename/remove unrelated parts without approval.
- **Cursor = Technical Supervisor** — inspects, challenges, verifies. Not the primary builder unless the owner explicitly instructs Cursor to implement.
- **Project Owner = Final Authority** — product, design, brand, priorities, UX, final approval.

## Golden rule

Claude builds. Cursor inspects, challenges, and verifies. Claude fixes what Cursor confirms is wrong. Cursor does the final review.

A task is complete only after: (1) Claude implements, (2) Claude tests, (3) Cursor reviews, (4) problems corrected, (5) Cursor confirms acceptance criteria, (6) owner visually approves when UI is involved.

Never treat Claude’s claim of completion as completion.

## Workflow

1. **Inspect** — Both tools inspect real code (pages, nav, reuse, state, DB, APIs, auth, styles, deps, tests, mobile). No guessing.
2. **Cursor pre-review** — Before Claude edits: relevant files, existing behavior, root cause, risks, reuse, do-not-touch, DB/API impact, security, mobile, measurable acceptance criteria. No vague “improve/clean/fix UI” feedback — cite exact files/functions/behaviors.
3. **Claude plan** — Short plan: inspect/change/create files, reuse, DB/API, tests, risks. Smallest safe complete change. No project-wide rewrites.
4. **Claude implements** — Match approved design; preserve working features; reuse; no duplicates; no fake success; no placeholder buttons; loading/empty/success/error; mobile; authz on server; secrets out of client; migrations when needed.
5. **Cursor reviews the actual diff** — Classify: Blocker / Major / Minor / Suggestion. No approval with unresolved Blockers or Majors.
6. **Correction loop** — Cursor documents exact issue → Claude fixes only that → Cursor re-reviews. No unrelated drive-bys.

## Change control (needs explicit approval)

Do not: rebuild whole pages without justification; replace design system; change brand/colors/logos; rename working routes; delete DB fields; remove working features; major dependency upgrades; change auth architecture, payment logic, roles, or production env vars.

## Design / mobile / functional

- Treat approved UI as specification (layout, spacing, type, radius, buttons, icons, cards, colors, shadows, gradients, motion, crops, safe areas, bottom nav).
- No dead-end screens; every interactive control must respond or be honestly disabled.
- Content must not hide behind status bar, notch, keyboard, bottom nav, or home indicator.
- Verify: happy path, invalid input, empty, slow network, API failure, unauthorized/wrong role, repeated taps, back, refresh.

## Security / backend / performance

- Hiding a button is not security. Enforce authz server-side + DB policies.
- Migrations: safe defaults, nulls, indexes, FKs, policies, rollback, existing-data compatibility.
- Avoid duplicate fetches, leaks, infinite rerenders, oversized images/bundles. Slow-but-works is not done.

## Claude completion report (required)

Summary · Files changed · DB changes · Before/After behavior · Tests run + results · Manual verification steps · Known limitations · Risks. Never say “fully complete” without evidence (tests, build, backend verify, mobile check).

## Cursor review format (required)

Review Result · What Claude Changed · What Works · Problems Found (severity, path, component, problem, user impact, correction) · Regression Risks · Security · Performance · Required Corrections · Final Verification Checklist. Do not claim it works without inspecting/testing.

## Conflicts

Cite exact code + user impact + tradeoff. Prefer safest option that preserves working behavior. Owner decides product calls.

## Definition of done

Requested behavior exists; design matches; buttons/nav/back work; loading/empty/error work; mobile + keyboard OK; auth/permissions OK; data save/load OK; no secrets; no unrelated removals; no critical console errors; build + relevant tests pass; Claude documented; Cursor reviewed with no unresolved blockers/majors; owner approved visible result when UI-involved.

## Handoff template (every task)

Task Name · User Problem · Expected Result · Approved Design · Must Keep · Claude’s Responsibility · Cursor’s Responsibility · Acceptance Criteria · Out of Scope · Final Verification.
